```java
// @@@ If the map contains the specified key, then it also contains the value returned by the get method.
```

We need to express: if map contains the specified key (i.e., this.containsKey(key) is true), then the map also contains the value returned by get (i.e., this.containsValue(\result) is true). However note that get may return null for key not present or for null mapping. The spec says "If the map contains the specified key, then it also contains the value returned by the get method." If key exists, get returns associated value (could be null). If value is null, does containsValue(null) return true? In Java HashMap, containsValue(null) returns true if any mapping has null value. So if key maps to null, then containsValue(null) should be true (since that key maps to null). So the assertion is: this.containsKey(key) => this.containsValue(\result). Use \old? Not needed because result is after execution. The premise uses current state after method? The containsKey(key) after get should still hold (since get doesn't modify). So we can use this.containsKey(key) => this.containsValue(\result). That's the assert.

Make sure to use correct syntax: assert this.containsKey(key) => this.containsValue(\result);

